raylib 6.0, SDL backend, on iOS. Applied to the fetched source by
ios/apply-patch.cmake (CMakeLists.txt, IOS branch). Two things:

1. Retina. UIKit ignores the size passed to SDL_CreateWindow (a window is
   the whole screen) and with SDL_WINDOW_HIGH_PIXEL_DENSITY the drawable is
   nativeScale times the size in points. The SDL backend never fills
   raylib's screenScale matrix, so it would draw in the bottom-left third
   of the display and report the wrong screen size. UpdateWindowSizeSDL()
   reads both sizes from the window (screen = points, for input and layout;
   render = pixels) and sets screenScale, at start-up and on every resize
   event (SDL3 sends one in points and one in pixels; both are ignored in
   favour of asking the window).

2. Default framebuffer. EAGL has no framebuffer 0: the GL view owns one and
   SDL publishes its id as a window property. rlgl binds 0 whenever it
   returns to the screen (EndTextureMode) or unbinds after creating a
   render texture, which on iOS draws into nothing. RL_DEFAULT_FRAMEBUFFER
   is that id on iOS and 0 elsewhere. Likewise EAGL presents whichever
   renderbuffer is bound, and rlgl unbinds after creating a render
   texture's depth buffer, so SwapScreenBuffer() rebinds the view's first.

3. Audio session. miniaudio's default category is PlayAndRecord, which
   opens the microphone path; a game wants Ambient (playback only, mixed
   with other audio, obeys the mute switch), as SDL itself defaults to.
   PlayAndRecord also deadlocks the iOS Simulator's audio server.

4. Simulator audio. The iOS Simulator's audio server can hang on the
   RemoteIO unit's RPCs (initialize, start), and AudioToolbox then aborts
   the process rather than returning an error, so nothing above it can
   recover. Simulator builds run without an audio device unless the
   RAYLIB_SIMULATOR_AUDIO environment variable is set; a real iPad or
   iPhone runs the unmodified code.

